    35	---
    36	
    37	## Definition
    38	
    39	A topological space $(X, \mathcal{T})$ ([[def-topological-space]]) is **locally
    40	compact** when
    41	
    42	> every point of $X$ has a compact neighbourhood:
    43	
    44	that is, for every $x \in X$ there is a neighbourhood $N$ of $x$
    45	([[def-neighbourhood-top]]) that is a compact subset of $X$
    46	([[def-compact-space]], [[def-subspace-topology-top]]).
    47	
    48	**A neighbourhood need not be open here**, and that is what makes the condition
    49	the weak one it is meant to be: $N$ is required only to contain some open set
    50	containing $x$. Writing "compact **open** neighbourhood" instead would define a
    51	strictly stronger property, satisfied by no space in which a point has no compact
    52	open neighbourhood, $\mathbb{R}$ among them; and requiring the compact set merely
    53	to *contain* $x$ would define a property so weak that every space with a
    54	singleton has it, singletons being compact.
    55	
    56	**Every compact space is locally compact**, since $X$ itself is a neighbourhood
    57	of each of its points and is a compact subset of itself. The converse fails, and
    58	$\mathbb{R}^n$ is the standard witness.
    59	
    60	**What the condition says in a metric space.** Let $(X,d)$ be a metric space
    61	carrying its metric topology ([[def-metric-topology]], [[def-metrizable-space]]),
    62	with balls as in [[def-metric-ball]], and let $x \in X$. Then
    63	
    64	> $x$ has a compact neighbourhood **if and only if** there are a real $r > 0$ and
    65	> a compact $K \subseteq X$ with $B(x,r) \subseteq K$.
    66	
    67	*Both directions are immediate and are discharged here.* If $N$ is a compact
    68	neighbourhood of $x$, fix an open $U$ with $x \in U \subseteq N$; by
    69	[[def-metric-topology]] there is $r > 0$ with $B(x,r) \subseteq U \subseteq N$, so
    70	$K := N$ serves. Conversely, if $B(x,r) \subseteq K$ with $K$ compact, then $K$
    71	contains the open set $B(x,r)$, which contains $x$, so $K$ is a neighbourhood of
    72	$x$ and is compact. Compactness of a subset of $(X,d)$ means the same thing read
    73	metrically and read topologically
    74	([[thm-compactness-agrees-with-metric-compactness]]), so the criterion may be
    75	applied with either development's theorems.
    76	
    77	**$\mathbb{R}^n$ is locally compact for every $n \ge 1$.** Give $\mathbb{R}^n$
    78	the product topology, which is the metric topology of the Euclidean metric $d_2$
    79	([[lem-metrics-on-rn]], [[cor-heine-borel-in-the-product-topology]]). For
    80	$p \in \mathbb{R}^n$ the set
    81	
    82	$$Q_p \;:=\; \{\, x \in \mathbb{R}^n : d_2(x,p) \le 1 \,\}$$
    83	
    84	is closed, being the complement of the union of the open balls $B(y, d_2(y,p) - 1)$
    85	over the points $y$ with $d_2(y,p) > 1$, and it is bounded
    86	([[def-metric-bounded-diameter]]), lying inside $B(p, 2)$; so $Q_p$ is compact by
    87	[[cor-heine-borel-in-the-product-topology]]. It contains the open ball $B(p,1)$,
    88	which contains $p$, so it is a compact neighbourhood of $p$. The space
    89	$\mathbb{R}^n$ is not compact, so local compactness is strictly weaker than
    90	compactness.
    91	
    92	## Remarks
    93	
    94	**Local compactness is a local condition and compactness is not.** The definition
    95	quantifies over points and asks for something in a neighbourhood of each; nothing
    96	is asserted about covers of the whole space. That is why a locally compact space
    97	may be as large as one likes, and why the two properties separate.
    98	
    99	**Where the extra strength is needed.** For an arbitrary space, "every point has
   100	a compact neighbourhood" does not by itself give a *base* of compact
   101	neighbourhoods at each point, nor an open set with compact closure around each
   102	compact set. Both of those do follow once the space is also Hausdorff, and that
   103	is [[thm-locally-compact-hausdorff-basics]]; several authors build the stronger
   104	condition into the definition and then note the agreement in the Hausdorff case.
   105	This library takes the weak definition and proves the strengthening under the
   106	hypothesis that licenses it.
   107	
   108	**Local compactness is not hereditary**, unlike metrizability. A subspace of a
   109	locally compact space need not be locally compact, and
   110	[[fs-local-compactness-is-hereditary]] records the failure with a witness; what
   111	does survive is heredity along open and along closed subspaces of a locally
   112	compact **Hausdorff** space ([[thm-locally-compact-hausdorff-basics]]).

