    36	
    37	## Definition
    38	
    39	Let $(X,d)$ be a metric space ([[def-metric-space]]).
    40	
    41	A subset $U \subseteq X$ is **open in $(X,d)$** if for every $x \in U$ there is a
    42	real $r > 0$ with $B(x,r) \subseteq U$ ([[def-metric-ball]]). A subset
    43	$F \subseteq X$ is **closed in $(X,d)$** if its complement $X \setminus F$ is
    44	open.
    45	
    46	The collection
    47	
    48	$$\mathcal{T}_d := \{\, U \subseteq X : U \text{ is open in } (X,d) \,\}$$
    49	
    50	of all open subsets is the **metric topology** of $d$ on $X$. A subset of $X$
    51	that is both open and closed is called **clopen**.
    52	
    53	**Two sets are open for trivial reasons.** $\emptyset$ is open, because the
    54	defining condition quantifies over no points; and $X$ is open, because
    55	$B(x,r) \subseteq X$ for every $x$ and every $r > 0$. Consequently $X$ and
    56	$\emptyset$ are also closed, and both are clopen.
    57	
    58	**A neighbourhood of a point $x$** is any open set containing $x$. The condition
    59	above therefore reads: $U$ is open exactly when every point of $U$ has a ball
    60	around it inside $U$, and it is the balls alone that have to be tested.
    61	
    62	**The metric, not the set, determines $\mathcal{T}_d$.** Two metrics on the same
    63	set may have different metric topologies, and two different metrics may have the
    64	same one; the systematic comparison is [[def-equivalent-metrics]].
    65	
    66	## Remarks
    67	
    68	- **What "topology" means here.** $\mathcal{T}_d$ is defined above as a
    69	  collection of subsets of $X$; the abstract notion of a topological space, a
    70	  collection of subsets closed under arbitrary unions and finite intersections
    71	  taken as primitive data, is introduced on a later page and is not used
    72	  here. What is proved
    73	  here is that $\mathcal{T}_d$ has exactly those closure properties
    74	  ([[thm-metric-open-set-algebra]]), which is what licenses the word.
    75	- **Open and closed are not opposites.** A set may be neither ($[0,1)$ inside
    76	  $\mathbb{R}$, once the usual metric is available from
    77	  [[lem-real-line-is-a-metric-space]]) or both ($\emptyset$ and $X$ always, and
    78	  in some spaces every subset at once, as the discrete metric on the companion
    79	  page shows). "Not open" is never a synonym for "closed".
    80	- **Closedness is complementation, and nothing else, at this stage.** The
    81	  description of closed sets by limits of sequences, and the description of the
    82	  closure as an infimum of distances, are theorems proved later on this page
    83	  ([[thm-metric-closure-characterisation]], [[thm-metric-sequential-closure]]),
    84	  not part of the definition.

