    38	**False claim:** local compactness ([[def-locally-compact-space]]) is a
    39	hereditary property ([[def-hereditary-property]]): every subspace
    40	([[def-subspace-topology-top]]) of a locally compact space is locally compact.
    41	
    42	**Where the claim comes from, and what is actually true.** Metrizability is
    43	hereditary, and so are several other properties of the same shape, so the
    44	expectation is natural. What is true for local compactness is heredity along
    45	**open** subspaces and along **closed** subspaces of a locally compact Hausdorff
    46	space; an arbitrary subspace need not inherit it. The witness below is the rationals inside the real line,
    47	which is neither open nor closed in it.
    48	
    49	## Facts & Assumptions
    50	
    51	**Given:** The real line $\mathbb{R}$ with its usual topology, the metric $d_{\mathbb{R}}(s,t) = |s-t|$, the subset $\mathbb{Q} \subseteq \mathbb{R}$ of rationals with the subspace topology, and the bounded open intervals $(c,d)$.
    52	
    53	[A1] The false claim: every subspace of a locally compact space is locally compact.
    54	
    55	[L1] $\mathbb{R}$ with its usual topology is metrizable, its open sets being exactly the sets $U$ such that every $x \in U$ has $(x-r, x+r) \subseteq U$ for some real $r > 0$ ([[lem-real-line-is-a-metric-space]], claim 3; [[def-metric-topology]], [[def-metrizable-space]], [[def-interval]], [[def-topological-space]]).
    56	
    57	[L2] $\mathbb{R}$ is locally compact: for $p \in \mathbb{R}$ the set $[p-1, p+1]$ is closed and bounded, hence a compact subset of the metric space $\mathbb{R}$ ([[thm-heine-borel-rn]], claim 3) and so a compact subset of the topological space $\mathbb{R}$ ([[thm-compactness-agrees-with-metric-compactness]], claim 2); and it contains the open $(p-1, p+1) \ni p$, so it is a neighbourhood of $p$ ([[def-neighbourhood-top]], [[def-locally-compact-space]]).
    58	
    59	[L3] For reals $c < d$ there is a rational strictly between them ([[lem-of-q-dense]]), and there is also an irrational strictly between them, the irrationals being dense in $\mathbb{R}$ ([[lem-q-and-irrationals-dense-r]], claim 2; [[def-interior-closure-boundary-top]]).
    60	
    61	[L4] A subset $A$ of a space is a compact subset when the subspace it carries is compact, and for $A \subseteq S \subseteq X$ the topology $A$ inherits from $S$ is the one it inherits from $X$, so compactness of $A$ does not depend on which of the two it is read in ([[def-compact-space]], [[def-subspace-topology-top]], [[def-hereditary-property]]).
    62	
    63	[L5] A compact subset of the metric space $\mathbb{R}$ is closed in $\mathbb{R}$ and bounded ([[thm-heine-borel-rn]], claim 3), and a closed subset of $\mathbb{R}$ contains every point of $\mathbb{R}$ all of whose neighbourhoods meet it ([[thm-closure-characterisation-top]], claim 1; [[def-interior-closure-boundary-top]]).
    64	
    65	## Refutation
    66	
    67	**Proof technique:** contradiction.
    68	
    69	1.1 Suppose the claim [A1] holds, so that every subspace of a locally compact space is locally compact. [A1, assume-contra]
    70	
    71	1.2 $\mathbb{R}$ with its usual topology is locally compact by [L2]. [L1, L2]
    72	
    73	2.1 By [A1] and step 1.2 the subspace $\mathbb{Q}$ would be locally compact, so the point $0$ of $\mathbb{Q}$ would have a compact neighbourhood $K$ in $\mathbb{Q}$: a set $K \subseteq \mathbb{Q}$, compact as a subspace, containing a set open in $\mathbb{Q}$ that contains $0$. By [L1] and the definition of the subspace topology that open set contains $(-\varepsilon, \varepsilon) \cap \mathbb{Q}$ for some real $\varepsilon > 0$, so $(-\varepsilon, \varepsilon) \cap \mathbb{Q} \subseteq K$. [A1, L1, L4, step 1.2]
    74	
    75	3.1 By [L4] the set $K$ is a compact subset of $\mathbb{R}$ as well, and hence closed in $\mathbb{R}$ and bounded by [L5]. [L4, L5, step 2.1]
    76	
    77	4.1 By [L3] there is an irrational $t$ with $0 < t < \varepsilon$. Every neighbourhood of $t$ in $\mathbb{R}$ contains an interval $(c,d)$ with $c < t < d$, which may be shrunk so that $0 < c$ and $d < \varepsilon$, and [L3] then puts a rational $q$ with $c < q < d$ in it; that $q$ lies in $(-\varepsilon,\varepsilon) \cap \mathbb{Q} \subseteq K$. So every neighbourhood of $t$ meets $K$, and $K$ being closed, [L5] gives $t \in K \subseteq \mathbb{Q}$ — but $t$ is irrational. This contradiction refutes the claim [A1]. [A1, L1, L3, L5, step 2.1, step 3.1, discharge-contradiction] ∎
    78	
    79	## Remarks
    80	
    81	**Why $\mathbb{Q}$ fails at every point, not just at $0$.** The argument uses nothing about $0$ beyond its being rational: for any $q \in \mathbb{Q}$ a compact neighbourhood would have to be a closed subset of $\mathbb{R}$ containing all rationals near $q$, and hence would contain the irrationals near $q$ as well, which it cannot. So $\mathbb{Q}$ is nowhere locally compact, and the witness is not an isolated defect at one point.
    82	
    83	**What the failure is about.** A compact subset of $\mathbb{R}$ is closed in $\mathbb{R}$, and a subset of $\mathbb{Q}$ that is closed in $\mathbb{R}$ has empty interior in $\mathbb{R}$; so no compact subset of $\mathbb{Q}$ can contain a whole interval's worth of rationals. The two facts pull in opposite directions, and $\mathbb{Q}$ is caught between them precisely because it is dense in $\mathbb{R}$ and is not all of it.
    84	
    85	**Heredity does hold in two special cases** and they are proved rather than assumed: along open subspaces of a locally compact Hausdorff space and along closed subspaces of any locally compact space ([[thm-locally-compact-hausdorff-basics]], claim 2). The set $\mathbb{Q}$ is neither open nor closed in $\mathbb{R}$, so it escapes both.

